Kirara Server APP Software License Agreement
Important Notice: This License Agreement (the "Agreement") is a legal agreement between you and the Kirara Server development team regarding the use of the Kirara Server software. Please read all the terms of this Agreement carefully before installing, copying, or using the Software.

Article 1: Definitions
1.1 Software means the Kirara Server APP, including all of its components, modules, built-in Schemes, interfaces, icons, documentation, explanatory materials, and all updates, patches, and upgrade packages provided by the Developer, regardless of the form in which they are delivered (installer, archive, or source code).

1.2 Services means the various remote services accessed through the Software after creating an "Instance" and binding it to a "Scheme", including but not limited to media playback (Kirara Media), live streaming (Kirara Live), cloud storage (Kirara Team Shared Cloud), email (Kmail), team collaboration (Chat), virtualization platforms (Vmm/ESXi), remote desktop (Winserver series), and OpenVPN virtual private network. The specific Schemes available are those actually provided within the Software.

1.3 Server means the Kirara Server cloud platform operated by the Developer, which provides online services such as account authentication, template distribution, comment storage, notification push, feed cards, scheme configuration synchronization, and avatar and background resources.

1.4 User means any natural person or legal entity that installs and uses the Software and is a party to this Agreement. Developer means the Kirara Server development team and its authorized entities. Content means the data that you create, upload, manage, or access through the Software, including instance configurations, token information, comments, files, and media.

Article 2: Grant of License
2.1 Provided that you comply with all the terms of this Agreement, the Developer grants you a non-exclusive, non-transferable, non-sublicensable, revocable personal license to install, copy, and use the Software on your own devices. The license term commences upon installation of the Software and continues until the termination of this Agreement.

2.2 This license does not include any right to access, modify, or distribute the source code of the Software, unless such rights are separately granted by an applicable open-source license (such as the MIT License). The main body of the Software is released as open source under the MIT License, and the rights you enjoy under that license are not restricted by this Agreement. However, the services you connect to through the Software, the server resources you access, and the non-open-source portions of the Software (including certain scheme adaptation logic, the encrypted cache mechanism, and the server communication protocol) remain subject to this Agreement and the applicable terms of service.

2.3 The Software may bundle or link to third-party open-source components and programs (such as OpenVPN, WebView2, etc.), the use of which is also subject to their own licenses. In the event of any conflict between this Agreement and a third-party license, this Agreement shall be construed in a manner that does not deprive the rights granted under such third-party license.

2.4 Except for the rights expressly granted in this Agreement, the Developer reserves all rights in the Software and its derivative works (including copyrights, trademarks, patents, and other intellectual property rights). Any rights not expressly granted to you under this Agreement are deemed reserved by the Developer.

Article 3: Restrictions on Use
3.1 You may not rent, lend, sell, or resell the Software, its Instances, Tokens, or Schemes, nor use them for commercial operation or hosting services for others, unless otherwise permitted in writing by the Developer.

3.2 You may not decompile, disassemble, reverse engineer, crack, circumvent license verification, or attempt to extract the source code of the Software, except for interoperability scenarios expressly permitted by applicable law. You may not remove, alter, or obscure any copyright notices, trademark identifiers, or license information contained in the Software.

3.3 You may not use the Software for any activity that violates the laws and regulations of the People's Republic of China or the laws of your jurisdiction, including but not limited to: unauthorized access to others' systems and data, network attacks and penetration, distribution of malicious programs or harmful code, infringement of others' intellectual property rights and privacy, sending spam, and circumventing service access controls.

3.4 The Software provides an auto-login (token link) feature, which may be used only with accounts and services to which you have authorized access. You may not use this feature to bulk-collect others' credentials, gain unauthorized access, or engage in any form of account abuse. You shall bear all consequences arising from such unauthorized use.

Article 4: User Data and Privacy
4.1 Local Data. The Software stores instance configurations, scheme bindings, tokens, comments, notification records, and application settings locally on your device, in the Kirara folder under the system user data directory (%APPDATA%/Kirara). The local database is a single-file embedded database (LiteDB). This folder is retained by default when the Software is uninstalled; you may delete it manually to remove the data completely.

4.2 Encryption Protection. Sensitive token fields (account name, account password, API key, server address) and login credentials (RefreshToken) are stored encrypted using AES-256-GCM, with the encryption key protected by Windows DPAPI (current user scope). Resources cached from the Server, such as background images, avatars, template covers, scheme icons, and .ovpn configuration files, are also stored in an encrypted cache to prevent tampering. Please safeguard your Windows account and login credentials. The Developer shall not be liable for any disclosure resulting from others gaining access to your login session or unencrypted copies.

4.3 Server Communication. To provide account authentication (login/registration/auto-login), template retrieval, comment synchronization, notification push (real-time push via SignalR), feed cards, incremental scheme URL synchronization, avatar and background resource downloads, and OpenVPN configuration distribution, the Software communicates with the Server and transmits necessary account identifiers, session tokens, local version numbers, and content you voluntarily submit. Except for the necessary scenarios described above, the Software does not upload your local data without your operation.

4.4 Third-Party Service Data. The services you connect to through each Scheme (such as media libraries, cloud drives, and email) are independently operated by their respective providers. Your data within those services is governed by their own terms of service and privacy policies and is unrelated to the Software and the Developer.

Article 5: Network Services and Third-Party Services
5.1 Network Dependence. Online features such as account authentication, template retrieval, comment synchronization, notification push, and configuration synchronization depend on an internet connection and server availability. Network outages, carrier failures, server maintenance, or force majeure may render these features unavailable or delayed. The Developer does not guarantee that the online services will be provided perpetually or without interruption, nor shall it be liable for any resulting losses.

5.2 Independence of Third-Party Services. The third-party services pointed to by each Scheme (media, cloud drives, email, virtualization platforms, etc.) are independently provided and maintained by their operators. The Developer is not responsible for their content, availability, stability, security, or their handling of your data. When using third-party services, you should review and comply with their terms of service and privacy policies on your own.

5.3 Remote Connection Compliance. When using remote connection features such as OpenVPN, please ensure that your actions comply with the network management regulations of the target network and applicable laws. Some countries or regions may impose legal restrictions on the use of virtual private networks; you should verify the legality yourself and bear the corresponding responsibility. Network conditions, server-side configurations, and carrier policies may all affect connection quality. The Developer does not guarantee that connections will be stable, successful, or achieve a specific speed.

Article 6: System Permissions and Security
6.1 Permissions. Installing the Software and its components may require administrator privileges to register system services (KiraraVpnHelper, which launches OpenVPN on behalf of the application under the LocalSystem account), install/load virtual network adapter drivers (TAP/Wintun), and write to the Program Files directory. The above permissions are used only to the extent necessary for the normal functioning of the Software.

6.2 Channel Security. Please obtain the Software and its updates only from the Developer's official channels (official website, officially released installers, and update channels). The Developer assumes no responsibility for installers of unknown origin, cracked versions, or modified versions, which may contain malicious code, steal your credentials, or compromise system security.

6.3 Security Obligations. You bear full security responsibility for your account credentials (username, password, API keys, Tokens, and RefreshToken) and the access rights to the services you manage, including but not limited to setting strong passwords, not disclosing credentials to others, logging out promptly, and enabling auto-login protection on public devices. You shall bear all losses resulting from credential disclosure, data theft, or service abuse caused by your failure to safeguard such information.

6.4 Vulnerability Reporting. If you discover any security vulnerabilities or defects in the Software, you are welcome to report them through the channels published by the Developer. Please do not publicly disclose vulnerability details or exploit vulnerabilities to attack others' systems before the Developer has fixed them.

